<p>Henry Waldo Coe was born November 4, 1857, in Waupun, Wisconsin, to Dr. {% person "samuel-buel-coe" %}Samuel Buel Coe{% endperson %} and {% person "mary-jane-cronkhite-coe" %}Mary Jane Cronkhite Coe{% endperson %}. Henry was the eldest of six children. Three of the couple's four sons followed their father into medicine. Samuel was a pioneer Wisconsin physician and served as a Union Army surgeon during the Civil War.</p>

<p>The family moved to Morristown, Minnesota, when Henry was five. After high school, he studied liberal arts at the University of Minnesota, transferred to the University of Michigan at Ann Arbor for medicine, and completed part of his internship under his father.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/biographies/henry-waldo-coe/259-coe-hw-portrait-narrow.jpg" alt="Portrait of Dr. Henry Waldo Coe">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Father and son arrived by rail in Valley City, Dakota Territory, in spring 1879, bringing a foot-powered printing press secretly owned by railroad agent {% person "c-f-kindred" %}C. F. Kindred{% endperson %}. Despite seven years of railroad access, Valley City remained sparsely settled. Henry published the first <em>Northern Pacific Times</em> on May 12, 1879. Part newspaper and part promotional handbill, it praised the Sheyenne Valley. Settlers began arriving within months after copies circulated in the East.</p>

<p>Samuel remained in Valley City, helped organize its 1881 incorporation, and served on the first city commission. He also edited the newspaper until 1880.</p>

<p>Henry completed his medical education at Long Island College Hospital in New York in July 1880 and returned to Dakota Territory. He treated Northern Pacific construction crews through 1883 and then established a private practice in Mandan.</p>

<p>Coe was among North Dakota's first physicians but, like his father, pursued broader interests. Mandan's village trustees appointed him city clerk May 3, 1881. He served on the city's first school board and as mayor from 1886 through 1889 after election on the Knights of Labor ticket. In 1890, Coe became president of the North Dakota State Medical Association and superintendent of the State Board of Health.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/biographies/henry-waldo-coe/280-coe-ad-1883-mandan-pioneer.jpg" alt="Henry Waldo Coe advertisement in the Mandan Pioneer in 1883">
    <figcaption>Advertisement in the <em>Mandan Pioneer</em>, 1883</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Coe was elected in 1884 to the last Dakota Territorial Legislature before division of the territory. With {% person "seth-peterson" %}Seth Peterson{% endperson %}, editor of the Valley City newspaper Coe had founded, he sponsored legislation regulating medical licenses.</p>

<p>Coe married {% person "viola-mae-boley-coe" %}Viola Mae Boley{% endperson %} on June 20, 1882. Her family was among the region's earliest pioneer settlers. Viola played organ music at Mandan's first Independence Day celebration using an instrument her father, {% person "elijah-boley" %}Elijah Boley{% endperson %}, had brought from Ohio.</p>

<p>Coe met {% person "theodore-roosevelt" %}Theodore Roosevelt{% endperson %} in 1884 while Roosevelt was in the region recovering his health. Roosevelt served as a Morton County deputy sheriff and frequently traveled to the county seat in Mandan. He also lived within Coe's territorial legislative district. Their friendship continued until Roosevelt's death in 1919.</p>

<p>The Coe family primarily lived at 203 Second Street Northwest. A stable behind the house kept the doctor's horses and buggy ready for emergency trips into the countryside.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/biographies/henry-waldo-coe/458-coe-hospital-ad.gif" alt="Advertisement for Henry Waldo Coe's Morningside Hospital">
    <figcaption>Advertisement in the <em>American Journal of Insanity</em></figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Henry and Viola moved to Portland, Oregon, in 1891. He specialized in nervous and mental illnesses and owned Morningside Hospital. In January 1893, he founded <em>The Medical Sentinel</em>, an independent medical magazine.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/biographies/henry-waldo-coe/334-coe-1925-lange-sweden-mine.jpg" alt="Henry Coe with Robert and Minnie Lange and mine workers around 1906">
    <figcaption>Coe, center with hat, with {% person "robert-lange" %}Robert{% endperson %} and {% person "minnie-lange" %}Minnie Lange{% endperson %} and mine workers, circa 1906</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Coe also owned and operated Mount St. Helens Consolidated Mining Company. Commercial quantities of copper, gold, silver, and nonmetallic minerals were discovered east of Spirit Lake beneath Mount St. Helens. Regional mining peaked between 1902 and 1908, and the Mount St. Helens Visitor Center later highlighted Coe's involvement.</p>

<p>Coe served in the Oregon Senate in 1894 and later as Oregon's U.S. senator. He led the City and County Medical Association and taught medicine at Willamette University in Salem. A national Progressive Party leader, he served as president of Oregon's Roosevelt League. Henry and Viola were invited to the White House on several occasions by Presidents Theodore Roosevelt and {% person "william-howard-taft" %}William Howard Taft{% endperson %}.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/biographies/henry-waldo-coe/334-tr-statute-1935-lutz-kms.jpg" alt="Theodore Roosevelt statue donated by Henry Coe to Mandan">
    <figcaption>Mandan's Theodore Roosevelt statue</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/biographies/henry-waldo-coe/334-tr-statue-portland-mod.jpg" alt="Theodore Roosevelt statue donated by Henry Coe to Portland">
    <figcaption>Portland's Theodore Roosevelt statue</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Coe commissioned sculptor {% person "alexander-phimister-proctor" %}Alexander Phimister Proctor{% endperson %} to create a statue of Theodore Roosevelt and donated it to Portland. In 1924, he gave Mandan a smaller version of the Rough Rider statue, now standing before the Northern Pacific depot. He also donated Portland statues of {% person "george-washington" %}George Washington{% endperson %} by {% person "pompeo-coppini" %}Pompeo Coppini{% endperson %}, {% person "abraham-lincoln" %}Abraham Lincoln{% endperson %} by {% person "george-fite-waters" %}George Fite Waters{% endperson %}, and {% person "joan-of-arc" %}Joan of Arc{% endperson %} by {% person "emmanuel-fremiet" %}Emmanuel Frémiet{% endperson %}.</p>

<p>Henry and Viola had three sons: {% person "george-clifford-coe" %}George Clifford{% endperson %}, born in Mandan in 1885; {% person "wayne-walter-coe" %}Wayne Walter{% endperson %}, born in Portland in 1894; and {% person "earl-alphonso-coe" %}Earl Alphonso{% endperson %}, born in Portland in 1896. Viola earned a medical degree from Woman's Hospital Medical College of Chicago and concentrated on philanthropy in Oregon. In 1907, the couple joined a group sent to Panama to report to President Roosevelt on working conditions during canal construction.</p>

<p>After a public divorce, Coe married {% person "elsie-ara-waggoner" %}Elsie Ara Waggoner{% endperson %} of Los Angeles on March 25, 1915. He retired from active practice in 1920, and the couple traveled extensively through Europe, Africa, Asia, and South America.</p>

<p>Coe died February 15, 1927, while receiving medical treatment in Glendale, California. At his Portland funeral, flowers reportedly arrived from every state.</p>

